# General

## Principles

- Prefer readability over clever code.
- Avoid overengineering; keep solutions simple (KISS).
- Do not duplicate logic — extract only when a second consumer exists (DRY).
- Use early returns to keep control flow flat.
- Keep each file focused on one responsibility.
- Match neighboring code: same form lib (RHF / Formik), same UI kit (Chakra / SCSS).
- Delivered code must not contain TODOs, placeholders, or incomplete implementations.
- During work, report any temporary WIP; remove it before delivery.
- Ask clarifying questions only when requirements are ambiguous.
- Do not expand scope (no extra refactors, docs, or comments unless asked).

## Workflow

1. Read neighboring files before writing new code — match patterns already in the module.
2. Search for an existing component, hook, or helper before creating a new one.
3. Implement the smallest change that satisfies the request.
4. Before finishing: run `npm run lint`, `npm run format`, relevant tests, and `npm run build`.
5. Run `npm run typecheck` when types changed; report pre-existing failures honestly.
6. Commit only when explicitly asked.

## Scope boundaries

- Fix only what the task requires. Do not rename unrelated files, reformat untouched code, or refactor adjacent modules.
- Do not add README, docs, or comments the user did not ask for.
- Do not introduce new libraries when the stack already covers the need (Chakra, react-query v3, Zustand, i18next).
- When editing legacy paths, prefer moving code toward `src/modules/<name>/` — but only for files you already touch.

## Verification checklist

| Change type | Required checks |
|---|---|
| Any code change | `lint`, `format`, `build` |
| Behavior change | focused or full tests |
| Types / API shapes | `typecheck` |
| UI-visible change | exercise loading, empty, error, mobile when possible |

